# `s-maxage` bounds the EDGE separately from the browser, and that split is
# the whole point. These filenames are content-hashed, so a browser can hold
# one forever and `max-age=31536000, immutable` says so. Cloudflare's edge is
# the part that cannot be trusted to hold one forever: a request that lands
# while a deploy is still propagating gets index.html back with a 200 (the SPA
# catch-all in _redirects answers anything Pages cannot serve as a file), and
# whatever the edge caches at a content-hashed URL is never revalidated,
# because the filename never changes. Observed live, twice: once as an
# unstyled site (a stylesheet served as text/html) and once on two favicons
# from a single deploy.
#
# 24 hours turns "poisoned until a human notices and purges" into "poisoned
# until tomorrow at worst", at no cost worth measuring -- the edge simply
# re-fetches from Pages, which is free, fast, and same-datacentre. Browsers
# still never re-ask.
#
# A URL purge is NOT a reliable fix for this, checked directly: purging the
# exact URL left the poisoned response in place three times running, and only
# `purge_everything` cleared it. The bad entry is keyed on the `Origin` header
# a browser sends and curl does not, so a single-file purge clears a different
# variant than the one being served.
# Security headers for every response. Deliberately declares no
# Cache-Control -- caching is per-asset-type and lives in the blocks below.
# Cloudflare Pages applies EVERY matching rule and merges them, so this block
# and the more specific ones underneath both take effect rather than one
# winning outright.
#
# No Content-Security-Policy here yet, on purpose, and its absence is the one
# thing in this file worth reading before adding it. A single site-wide
# `script-src 'self'` would break iPhone photo uploads: src/admin/heic.ts
# dynamically imports heic-to, whose decoder runs inside a Worker built from
# a blob URL, and a blob worker inherits the creating document's CSP rather
# than carrying one of its own. That decoder's emscripten glue builds its
# method callers through the dynamic-code constructor at runtime -- a live
# path, not a dead branch, checked in the shipped dist/assets/heic-to-*.js --
# so the only policy that lets it run is 'unsafe-eval' on whatever document
# created the worker. Granting that site-wide, to every visitor, to support
# one admin-only conversion is a worse trade than shipping the headers below
# now and splitting the policy by path afterwards.
#
# X-Content-Type-Options is not decoration on this site specifically: it has
# twice served a response under the wrong Content-Type through a poisoned
# edge cache (see the comment above), and sniffing is the step that turns a
# wrong Content-Type into executed script.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security carries no `preload` token, deliberately. Preload
# is a submission into a list compiled into browsers, it is slow and awkward
# to undo, and this is still running on a throwaway test host. `max-age` and
# `includeSubDomains` are reversible; preload effectively is not.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), magnetometer=(), gyroscope=(), accelerometer=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com; frame-src https://www.google.com; worker-src blob:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the CSP above is ONE policy and not two, and why it carries
# 'unsafe-eval'. Both were decided by measurement, not preference.
#
# The site would rather have a strict policy on the public pages and a looser
# one on /edit, because the loose part exists solely for the admin: picking an
# iPhone photo dynamically imports heic-to (src/admin/heic.ts), whose decoder
# runs inside a Worker built from a blob URL, and a blob worker inherits the
# creating document's CSP rather than carrying one of its own. That decoder's
# emscripten glue generates its method callers through the dynamic-code
# constructor at runtime -- a live path, not a dead branch, confirmed in the
# shipped dist/assets/heic-to-*.js. So the admin needs both `worker-src blob:`
# and the script-src token permitting runtime code generation.
#
# A per-path block cannot express that, and the reason is not obvious enough
# to leave unwritten. A probe header set in both a `/*` block and an `/edit`
# block was deployed to the real platform, and /edit came back carrying BOTH
# values. Cloudflare Pages APPENDS matching header rules; it does not replace
# them. For most headers that is harmless. For Content-Security-Policy it is
# decisive: a response carrying two CSP headers is enforced as their
# INTERSECTION, never as last-wins. So a strict `/*` policy plus a loose
# `/edit` policy does not loosen /edit at all -- it leaves the strict policy
# still blocking the decoder, while every header reads correctly in curl and
# the only symptom is that iPhone uploads stop working on the one device the
# owner actually uses.
#
# Inverting it (loose on `/*`, strict on each public route) does work, because
# intersection is then in the right direction. It was rejected for being
# silently fragile in exactly the way this project keeps getting bitten by: a
# new page added without remembering to add a block gets the LOOSE policy, and
# nothing anywhere fails.
#
# What 'unsafe-eval' actually costs here is small, and worth stating plainly
# rather than implying it is free. `script-src` still permits no inline
# script, no external script but Cloudflare's own analytics beacon, and no
# `javascript:` URL -- so an attacker cannot get code running in the first
# place, and 'unsafe-eval' only widens what code could do once it already is.
# The injection route this site actually had (a `javascript:` string in a
# content field reaching an href, see src/content/validate.ts) is blocked by
# script-src with or without it.
#
# scripts/check-csp.mjs verifies this policy against the production bundle in
# a real Chromium on every route, and probes the two admin-only capabilities
# directly, because no page load exercises them and their failure surfaces
# only when she picks a photo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# s-maxage was 86400 and is now 3600, because the bound it sets turned out to
# be the recovery time for a total outage rather than for a cosmetic one.
#
# When this reasoning was written, the two observed poisonings were a
# stylesheet and a pair of favicons -- annoying, survivable for a day. Since
# then the same mechanism hit the ENTRY BUNDLE: /assets/index-*.js was served
# as text/html, Chrome refused to execute it as a module script, and every
# route on the site rendered a blank white page. "Poisoned until tomorrow at
# worst" is a fine answer for an unstyled page and a bad one for a site that
# is simply gone, since the blast radius is not the asset -- it is everything.
#
# An hour costs nothing measurable. The edge re-fetches from Pages, which is
# free, fast and same-datacentre, and browsers are unaffected either way:
# max-age and immutable are untouched, so a visitor who already has these
# bytes never re-asks, and these filenames are content-hashed, so a new build
# is a new URL rather than a revalidation.
#
# This shortens recovery; it does not prevent the poisoning. The mechanism is
# the SPA catch-all in public/_redirects answering a not-yet-propagated asset
# with index.html at 200 -- see docs/cloudflare-cutover.md §16b for why
# _redirects cannot be made to exclude /assets/*, and `npm run verify:deploy`
# for the check that actually detects it.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable, s-maxage=3600

/*.webp
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/*.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/*.pdf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/build-info.json
  Cache-Control: no-store
